\name{stats_adjust}
\alias{stats_adjust}
\title{Benjamini-Hochberg adjust p-values, keeping every position}
\usage{
stats_adjust(p_values, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{p_values}{A numeric vector of p-values, each a fraction from 0 to 1, or \code{NA} for untested.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Adjust a list of p-values for the false discovery rate with Benjamini-Hochberg, keeping every input position and leaving untested entries out of the family.

For the i-th smallest of m p-values the adjusted value is the minimum over j >= i of
p_(j) m / j, capped at 1 (Benjamini and Hochberg 1995), computed by mzLib's
\code{MultipleTesting.BenjaminiHochberg}. \code{NA} (or \code{NaN}) marks a feature that was not tested: it
stays \code{NA} and is \strong{not counted in m}, which is the honest family. Do not write 1 for an
untested feature; that enlarges m.
}
\details{
\code{\link{stats_fit}} already reports \code{bh_adjusted}; this is for p-values from anywhere else.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_adjusted}: \code{records}, a data.frame with row i for \code{p_values[i]} - \code{p_value}
  as read and \code{bh_adjusted}, both fractions (0 to 1), \code{NA} for an untested entry; \code{line_count}
  lines (values) given; \code{tested_count}, m, the p-values the adjustment is over; \code{row_count} rows;
  and \code{caveats}.

}
